    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Ceiling paint is not a marketing invention, but it is also not magic. It is a paint formulated around three problems specific to ceilings: spatter from rolling overhead, light raking across a large flat surface and the fact that nobody ever scrubs a ceiling. Wall paint is formulated around the opposite set of priorities. Knowing which trade-offs each one makes tells you when the swap is fine and when it will cost you.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ceiling paint is typically thicker, almost always flat, and formulated to roll on with less spatter and to hide surface imperfections under raking light. Wall paint is typically thinner, available in a range of sheens and formulated to be scrubbed, touched up and to stand up to hands, furniture and cleaning. Those design goals pull in different directions, which is why most paint lines sell both.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can put wall paint on a ceiling, and in many rooms it will look fine. You can also put ceiling paint on a wall, but it will usually scuff and mark more easily, and it may not take kindly to being washed. The swap matters most on large, smooth ceilings with strong window light, where a dedicated flat ceiling paint hides flaws that a wall paint with any sheen will reveal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price is usually a point in ceiling paint's favor. Many dedicated ceiling paints cost less per gallon than the same brand's premium wall paint, because they do not need the scrub-resistant resins that make good wall paint expensive. That makes ceiling paint a sensible default for ceilings, not just a nice-to-have.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are exceptions on both sides. A bathroom ceiling that gets wet and grows mildew may be better served by a moisture-resistant paint with a slight sheen than by a basic flat ceiling white. And a low ceiling in a busy space, such as a basement playroom, may get touched often enough that a washable wall paint makes more sense. The sections below go through each difference so you can decide.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
