                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical consistency</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thicker, higher viscosity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thinner, flows and levels more</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical sheen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat (sometimes matte)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat to semi-gloss</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spatter when rolled overhead</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Formulated to be low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scrub resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low priority</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher priority</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical colors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White, sometimes tinted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full color range</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price per gallon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often higher at the same tier</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="viscosity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Thickness and Spatter
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most noticeable difference when you open the can is consistency. Ceiling paints are generally made thicker so they cling to the roller and the ceiling instead of misting off as you roll overhead. That higher viscosity is what manufacturers mean when they describe a ceiling paint as low-spatter or drip-resistant. When you roll a thinner wall paint overhead, more of it comes off the roller as fine droplets that land on you, the floor and the walls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thickness also helps with sag. A heavy film of a thin paint on a ceiling can run or drip before it sets, especially near the edges where cutting in leaves a thicker band. A thicker ceiling paint holds in place better, which makes it more forgiving for people who load the roller generously, which is exactly what you should do to avoid streaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is leveling. Thinner paints flow out after they are rolled, so roller texture flattens before the paint sets. Thicker paints level less, so the roller texture stays. On a ceiling that is usually acceptable, and even helpful, because a slight uniform stipple diffuses light and hides small flaws. On a wall that will be seen up close, many people prefer the smoother finish of a better-leveling wall paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Higher solids content often comes with thickness, which can mean better hide per coat. That is one reason ceiling paints often cover in fewer coats than you might expect from their price. It is not guaranteed, and coverage varies widely between products, but it is part of why a good ceiling paint can be the better value overhead even when a premium wall paint is available.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHY IT MATTERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Spatter is a safety and cleanup issue.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rolling overhead puts your face directly under the roller. A low-spatter ceiling paint means less paint in your eyes and hair and on the floor, which is a practical reason to use one even in rooms where the finish difference would not show.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sheen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Sheen and Flaw Hiding
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every dedicated ceiling paint is flat, the lowest sheen there is. Flat paint scatters light rather than reflecting it, which is exactly what you want on a ceiling. Ceilings catch raking light from windows and fixtures across their whole surface, so any shine highlights drywall seams, patches, nail pops and roller marks. Flat hides them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wall paints come in flat, matte, eggshell, satin, semi-gloss and gloss. Many people choose eggshell or satin for walls because a little sheen makes them easier to wipe clean. Put that same eggshell on a ceiling and it will reflect window light along every seam and roller lap. On a large ceiling with a picture window, the difference between flat and eggshell can be the difference between a smooth ceiling and one that looks lumpy all afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flat also makes touch-ups easier. Because it reflects so little light, a small touch-up with the same flat paint usually blends invisibly. Touch-ups in eggshell and satin often show as shiny or dull spots, because the sheen changes slightly with how thickly the paint is applied. On a ceiling that you might need to patch after a leak or a fixture change, that is a real advantage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guide to the <a href="/ceiling-paint-sheen/">best sheen for ceiling paint</a> goes into when a matte or eggshell finish makes sense overhead, mainly in bathrooms and kitchens where moisture and grease are an issue. For most living spaces and bedrooms, flat is the right answer, and that is what most ceiling paints are.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sheen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hides flaws</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Wipeable</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical use overhead</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Least</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most ceilings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Matte</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very good</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ceilings wanting a little wipeability</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Eggshell</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some kitchens and baths</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Satin and above</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rare on ceilings</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Durability and Washability
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wall paint is built to be touched. Hands, furniture, vacuums, kids and pets all hit walls, and walls get washed. To survive that, good wall paints use more expensive resins that resist scrubbing and burnishing, which is when a flat surface turns shiny where it is rubbed. That durability is a big part of what you pay for in a premium wall paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceilings rarely get touched or washed, so ceiling paint does not need that durability, and most ceiling paints do not have much of it. Rub a basic flat ceiling paint with a wet cloth and it may mark, burnish or lift. That is not a defect; it is a trade-off the manufacturer made to keep the paint flat, thick and affordable.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the main reason not to use ceiling paint on walls in busy rooms. It will look fine on day one, but scuffs from shoes, furniture and hands will be hard to clean off, and scrubbing at them will make the area look worse. For walls, a dedicated wall paint is almost always worth the extra cost. Our <a href="/best-interior-paint/">interior paint picks</a> focus on wall paints that hold up to that kind of use.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are ceilings where washability matters. Kitchen ceilings above a cooktop collect grease, and bathroom ceilings collect moisture and sometimes mildew. In those rooms, a ceiling paint with mildew-resistant additives, or a moisture-resistant wall or bath paint in a low sheen, can be the better choice. Our <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">ceiling paint guide</a> notes which products are formulated for humid rooms.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TRADE-OFF</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Flatter usually means less washable.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The properties that make a paint hide flaws overhead are the same ones that make it mark easily. That trade-off is fine on a ceiling nobody touches and a problem on a wall everyone does.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="color-change" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Color-Change and Stain-Blocking Ceiling Paints
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some ceiling paints add features wall paint rarely has. The best known is color change: the paint goes on tinted, often pink or purple, and dries to white. That makes it easy to see where you have already rolled, which is surprisingly difficult when you are putting white over white on a ceiling you are looking at from below. Our ceiling paint guide includes a color-change pick for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Others include stain-blocking or mildew-resistant formulas. Stain-blocking ceiling paints are designed to cover light water stains and discoloration that would bleed through ordinary paint, though severe stains still need a dedicated primer. Mildew-resistant paints include additives that inhibit growth on the paint film in humid rooms, which matters most on bathroom and laundry room ceilings.
          </p>

          <p>
            These features are worth having when they solve a problem you actually have. If you are refreshing a clean living room ceiling, color change is a convenience and stain-blocking is unnecessary. If you are painting over an old water stain in a hallway, stain-blocking is important, and if it is a bathroom with poor ventilation, mildew resistance is the feature to prioritize.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wall paints sometimes offer similar options, particularly bath-specific formulas with mildew resistance and a satin or eggshell sheen. Those are a reasonable choice for a bathroom ceiling, especially a small one where light is not raking across a large flat surface and the sheen is less likely to show flaws.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Ceiling Paint Features at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Flat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical sheen</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Low</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Spatter, by design</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Tint</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Color-change option</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">White</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most common color</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="swap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can You Swap One for the Other?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Using wall paint on a ceiling is common and usually fine, especially if it is a flat or matte wall paint. Expect more spatter when rolling, so cover the floor well, wear glasses and load the roller a little lighter. Expect possibly more coats if the wall paint is thinner. And avoid eggshell or satin on a large ceiling with strong window light, because the sheen will show every flaw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Using a flat wall paint on the ceiling has one real advantage: if you are painting the walls and ceiling the same color, you only need one can and there is no visible line at the junction. Many people paint ceilings in a tinted color to match or complement the walls, and in that case a wall paint in a flat or matte sheen is the obvious choice, since most ceiling paints come only in white or a narrow range of tints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Using ceiling paint on walls is generally a poor idea except in rooms nobody touches. It will scuff and mark easily, it will not wash well, and the flat finish will burnish where it is rubbed. A closet interior or a ceiling-height soffit is fine; a hallway, kitchen or child's bedroom is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leftover ceiling paint can also be useful as a base coat. Some painters use it as an inexpensive first coat on new drywall walls after priming, then finish with a proper wall paint. That works, but it is not a substitute for a real primer on bare drywall, and it is only worthwhile if you already have the paint on hand. Our <a href="/best-primer/">primer guide</a> covers what new drywall actually needs.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
