        <div id="choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Choose for Your Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a standard ceiling in a living room, bedroom or hallway, buy a dedicated flat ceiling paint. It will roll on with less mess, hide more flaws and often cost less than a wall paint of similar quality. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-ceiling/">how to paint a ceiling</a> covers the method, which matters as much as the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a ceiling you want tinted to match or complement the walls, use a flat or matte wall paint in the chosen color. You lose a bit of spatter resistance and possibly some flaw hiding, but you gain the color, and on most ceilings the difference is small. Roll carefully, keep a wet edge and plan on two coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a bathroom or kitchen ceiling, prioritize moisture or mildew resistance over sheen. A mildew-resistant ceiling paint is a good first choice; a bath-specific wall paint in matte or eggshell is a reasonable alternative, especially on a small ceiling. Make sure the room has an exhaust fan that actually runs, because no paint will survive a ceiling that stays wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a ceiling with stains, plan on a stain-blocking primer first regardless of which topcoat you choose. Stain-blocking ceiling paints help with light discoloration, but a water stain or smoke damage needs a dedicated primer to keep it from coming back. The rest of our <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">ceiling paint picks</a> cover the best flat, low-spatter, color-change and mildew-resistant options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BOTTOM LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ceiling paint for ceilings, wall paint for walls.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Swap only when you have a reason: a tinted ceiling, a damp bathroom or leftover paint you want to use. Otherwise each product is cheaper and better at its own job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling Eggshell Wall Paint on a Big Ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A little sheen is welcome on walls you clean, but on a large ceiling with window light it highlights every seam, patch and roller lap. If you use wall paint overhead, choose flat or matte. Save eggshell for small bathroom ceilings where moisture resistance outweighs looks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Ceiling Paint on High-Traffic Walls</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ceiling paint is not built to be scrubbed. On a hallway or kitchen wall it will scuff and burnish, and washing it makes marks worse. Use a proper wall paint where people touch the surface, and keep ceiling paint for ceilings, closets and other untouched areas.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Stain-Blocking Paint Replaces Primer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stain-blocking ceiling paints help with light discoloration, but a real water stain, smoke damage or nicotine will often bleed through anyway. Spot-prime those areas with a dedicated stain-blocking primer first, then apply the ceiling paint over the top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Thinning Ceiling Paint to Make It Roll Easier</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ceiling paint is thick on purpose. Thinning it increases spatter, reduces hide and can cause sagging and drips. If it feels too heavy, use a better roller cover and keep it fully loaded rather than adding water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Moisture in Bathrooms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A basic flat ceiling paint in a bathroom without good ventilation can grow mildew and peel. Choose a mildew-resistant formula and run the exhaust fan during and after showers. The fan matters as much as the paint.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use regular wall paint on my ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A flat or matte wall paint works well on most ceilings, and it is the obvious choice if you want the ceiling tinted to match the walls. Expect a little more spatter and possibly an extra coat. Avoid higher sheens on large ceilings with strong light, because they show flaws.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use ceiling paint on walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it is rarely a good idea except in closets or areas nobody touches. Ceiling paint is formulated for flaw hiding and low spatter, not scrub resistance, so it scuffs and marks easily and does not clean well. Use a dedicated wall paint for walls.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is ceiling paint thicker than wall paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally, yes. Ceiling paints are usually formulated with higher viscosity so they cling to the roller and the ceiling instead of spattering or dripping. Thickness varies between products, but it is one of the defining differences between the two.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is ceiling paint always flat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flat paint scatters light rather than reflecting it, which hides drywall seams, patches and roller marks on a surface that catches raking light from windows and fixtures. Since ceilings are rarely touched or washed, the lower durability of flat paint is not a problem there.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is ceiling paint cheaper than wall paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often it is, within the same brand and quality tier. Ceiling paint does not need the expensive scrub-resistant resins that durable wall paint requires. That makes it a sensible choice for ceilings, where you get better flaw hiding and less spatter for less money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need ceiling paint for a bathroom ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. A mildew-resistant ceiling paint is a good choice, but a bath-specific wall paint in a low sheen also works, especially on a small ceiling. The important features are moisture and mildew resistance, along with an exhaust fan that runs during and after showers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you tint ceiling paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some ceiling paints can be tinted to light colors, but the range is usually limited compared with wall paint. If you want a strong color on the ceiling, a flat or matte wall paint in that color is usually the easier and more reliable route.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

